.TH OC-SEARCH 1 "2026-08-25" "oc-search" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
oc-search \- search opencode conversation history with ripgrep and fzf
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B oc-search
[\fIquery\fP]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B oc-search
is a fish script that searches opencode conversation history. It dumps
session messages from the opencode SQLite database into per-session
markdown files under
.IR ~/.cache/oc-search/ ,
then lets you filter and open the cached sessions with
.BR fzf (1).
.P
The database is read from
.IR ~/.local/share/opencode/opencode.db .
Only non-archived sessions are indexed. The fzf list is sorted by last
update time ascending, so the oldest conversation is at the top and the
newest at the bottom. If the database does not exist, or either
.B rg
or
.B fzf
is not installed, the script prints an error to stderr and exits with
status 1.
.P
The optional first argument is used as the initial
.B fzf
query. Since
.B fzf
starts in
.B \-\-disabled
mode, typing after launch re-runs
.B rg
and live-refilters the session list.
.SS Cache
Each session is cached as a file named
.RI UPDATED::ID::TITLE
in
.IR ~/.cache/oc-search/ ,
where
.B UPDATED
is the session's last-update time in milliseconds since the epoch (used
to sort the list oldest-to-newest) and
.B ID
is the opencode session ID.
The title has path-hostile characters replaced with underscores and is
truncated to 80 characters. Each file begins with a header of the title
and the local creation time, followed by the conversation with
.B "## USER"
and
.B "## ASSISTANT"
role markers, per-message timestamps, and the raw message text (markdown
bold markers are stripped).
.P
The fzf list shows only the title (the
.B UPDATED
and
.B ID
prefixes are hidden), with the oldest conversation at the top.
.P
A session is re-dumped when its cached file is older than the session's
update time. User messages that begin with
.B [SUPERMEMORY]
or
.B <supermemory-recall>
are skipped. A
.B .version
marker inside the cache directory tracks the cache format version; when
it differs from the script's version, all cached files are deleted and
rebuilt.
.SS Preview
The fzf preview window (right 60%, wrapped) runs
.B rg
with case-insensitive context (3 lines around the query) over the
selected file, falling back to
.BR bat (1)
with plain style and markdown syntax, then
.BR cat (1).
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B query
Optional positional argument. Sets the initial
.B fzf
query. There are no command-line flags.
.SH KEYBINDINGS
.TP
.B ENTER
Open the selected session file in
.BR nvim (1).
.TP
.B CTRL-O
Start the supermemory user service and open the conversation in
.B opencode
with
.IR opencode\ \-s ,
using the session ID embedded in the filename (the second
.B ::
field).
.TP
.B CTRL-X
Delete the selected conversation. Pressing
.B CTRL-X
once arms the deletion for the selected conversation (the header shows
a \*(lqpress again to confirm delete\*(rq hint); pressing it again on the
same conversation removes the session, its messages and parts from the
database and deletes its cache file, so the conversation disappears from
the list. Pressing
.B CTRL-X
on a different conversation re-arms it to that one.
.TP
.B CTRL-C
Exit without selecting.
.SH EXAMPLES
Search with an initial query, then refine by typing:
.IP
.EX
oc-search vaapi
.EE
.P
Search interactively:
.IP
.EX
oc-search
.EE
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.local/share/opencode/opencode.db
The opencode conversation database.
.TP
.I ~/.cache/oc-search/
Cache directory holding the session files and the
.B .version
marker.
.TP
.I ~/.cache/oc-search/.pending
Temporary marker written by the
.B CTRL-X
deletion flow to record which conversation is armed for deletion.
.TP
.I ~/.local/bin/oc-search-delete
Helper script that arms and performs conversation deletion. The default
database and cache paths can be overridden with the
.B OC_SEARCH_DB
and
.B OC_SEARCH_CACHE
environment variables.
.TP
.I ~/.local/bin/oc-search-header
Helper script that renders the dynamic
.B fzf
header showing the pending-delete state. The cache path can be
overridden with
.B OC_SEARCH_CACHE.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR rg (1),
.BR fzf (1),
.BR sqlite3 (1),
.BR bat (1)
